= Operating system — Technical =

== Core abstractions ==
Operating systems expose abstractions such as:

<pre>
process / thread
virtual address space
file / descriptor
socket
device
user / protection domain
</pre>

These virtualize and multiplex underlying resources.

== Scheduling ==
Schedulers allocate CPU time subject to goals such as throughput, latency, fairness, deadlines or energy.

Policy differs across interactive, batch and real-time systems.

== Virtual memory ==
Page tables map virtual to physical addresses.

Demand paging, copy-on-write, shared mappings and replacement policies trade memory capacity, latency and isolation.

== Concurrency ==
Threads and processes interact through synchronization primitives, IPC, shared memory and message passing.

Race conditions, deadlock and starvation are central correctness hazards.

== Kernel structure ==
Monolithic, microkernel, hybrid and exokernel-style designs partition privileged mechanisms differently.

